---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const entries = [
  {
    day: 'WEEK 1: 09/14/2026',
    title: 'Defined the project direction.',
    text: 'I outlined an AI-assisted Pointwise Glyph workflow for mesh creation, refinement, and grid-convergence comparisons. I identified mesh qualification, near-wall y+, and solution changes across successively refined grids as the central technical focus.',
  },
  {
    day: 'WEEK 1: 09/15/2026',
    title: 'Built the portfolio foundation.',
    text: 'I reviewed classmate Hugh Cairney’s Astro and Tailwind setup, then created a light four-page site with a shared layout. I found the site creation incredibly quick once I narrowed down my stylistic preferences: compact labels, thin rules, square panels, and graph-paper-inspired log cards.',
  },
  {
    day: 'WEEK 1: 09/15/2026',
    title: 'Compared models for animation generation.',
    text: 'While building the portfolio Easter eggs, I experimented with both GPT-5.6 Terra and Claude Sonnet 4.6 for generating canvas animations. Claude produced noticeably cleaner results. This further interests me in understanding the optimal use cases of different models, particularly for visual and animation generation.',
  },
   {
       day: 'WEEK 2: 09/22/22026',
       title: 'Created a notation-consistency skill.',
       text: "This week I went back and forth on where to start: an engineering-reasoning skill, a technical-writing skill, or some kind of
 plotting-assistance skill. I ended up going with technical writing, mostly because I wanted to get a feel for how skills are actually structured and put
 together before taking on something more complicated. One problem I run into all the time, especially in longer fluid and solid mechanics documents, is
 keeping my notation consistent across a whole paper or thesis. So I built an equation and notation consistency checker, something that flags places where
 I've used different subscripts, symbols, or conventions for the same quantity. I started by explaining the problem to GPT-5.6 Luna and giving it a real
 example from my thesis that I'd fixed a few months ago, where I had used two different subscripts for vibrational temperature. From there I let it build out
 the rest of the skill and asked it to come up with a few more representative examples. I used those to tighten the instructions and cut down on false
 positives, especially cases where a notation difference was an intentional choice by the author and not actually a mistake. The biggest takeaway for me was
 that starting with a narrow, well-defined task made it much easier to figure out how to describe what a skill should do and where its limits should be.
 Unrelated, but I also spent a frustrating chunk of time this week dealing with Pi repeatedly telling me it didn't have access to certain directories or
 permission to write files. I still haven't figured out how to fix it globally. It kept happening after I quit Pi, restarted it in a new terminal, and
 updated it. From what I can tell, the write-permission problem isn't coming from Nono either, so that's something I'll need to dig into more.",
     },
];
---
<BaseLayout title="Dev log" description="Development notes for Madison Taylor Hobbs's AI research portfolio.">
  <section class="max-w-3xl">
    <p class="font-mono text-xs tracking-[0.14em] text-red-accent">DEVELOPMENT LOG</p>
    <h1 class="mt-5 font-display text-5xl font-medium tracking-[-0.06em] sm:text-6xl">Notes from the work.</h1>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl leading-7 text-graphite">A concise record of how this project and portfolio are taking shape, including what I asked AI tools to help with and what I reviewed myself.</p>

    <div class="mt-12 grid gap-5">
      {entries.map((entry) => (
        <article class="paper-grid border border-rule p-5 sm:p-7">
          <p class="font-mono text-xs tracking-[0.12em] text-red-accent">{entry.day}</p>
          <h2 class="mt-6 font-display text-2xl font-medium tracking-[-0.04em]">{entry.title}</h2>
          <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-7 text-graphite">{entry.text}</p>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
